WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:02.669
By now, surely you've heard of...

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:03.503
"Pickelball."

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.071
"-Pickleball. "
"-Pickleball!"

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:05.705
Right?

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:06.806
The so-called...

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.608
"...fastest growing sport in the country."

00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:11.878
Pickleball's popularity skyrocketed 
during the pandemic...

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.947
bringing it out of retirement communities

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:15.482
and into the mainstream.

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:16.649
Oh, my God.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.451
It is a phenomenon.

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:20.687
I discovered pickleball and a light went off in my head.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.789
Where would you be without pickleball right now?

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:24.457
I honestly don't know.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:26.826
Now there are fancy new 
Instagram ads

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.862
selling trendy paddles and balls...

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:32.732
a professional league backed by 
Kevin Durant and LeBron James...

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:36.569
and a Facebook group for pretty much 
every town in America.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:39.472
Everybody loves Pickleball.

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:41.908
Well, maybe not everybody.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:44.878
"Why don't you take that pickleball
and shove it up your ******!"

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:45.612
"A catastrophe..."

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.714
"The lantern flies of the sports world."

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.150
So how did pickleball take over?

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.485
And where can it go from here?

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:59.092
It's not hard to understand
pickleball's appeal.

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.528
It's sort of like a giant adult 
playground game

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.264
that's easy to pick up and 
find people to play with.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.699
Whenever we come out and 
it's actually nice weather

00:01:06.699 --> 00:01:09.869
the entire ground is covered 
with pickleball courts.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.305
I live literally like 
right next door here

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.607
and I just saw scores of people 
on the weekends and...

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.409
I kinda just jumped in.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.911
The rules are similar to tennis

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:20.113
but played on a much smaller court.

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:23.116
Which means less running 
and quicker games.

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:25.485
And pickleball can be played 
anywhere.

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:28.988
The nets are advertised as being 
portable and easy to assemble

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.124
and there's plenty of resources online

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.259
that show you how to carve up 
a tennis court

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:37.030
into one, two, or up to four 
pickleball courts.

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:41.468
In some places, the impromptu 
nature of the sport

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:42.769
has caused some friction

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.839
and so-called turf wars for public space.

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:48.741
Like at the Peninsula Tennis Club 
in San Diego

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:52.278
where a group of pickleball players 
staged a dramatic take over

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.814
demanding its tennis courts 
be converted for pickleball.

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:58.852
In Santa Rosa, public courts dual-lined 
for pickleball and tennis

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:00.420
were vandalized with motor oil.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.655
To send the message that the new craze

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.691
was no longer welcome there.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.993
But it's not just the fight for court space

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:09.562
that has some people fed up 
with pickleball.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.065
This is a sound level meter.

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:14.200
It measures sound pressure levels in decibels.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.936
A decibel is one unit of loudness

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:20.373
and it's measured logarithmically
rather than linearly.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.108
Basically, that means that

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:26.246
steadily increasing sound level by 10 decibels 
doesn't look like this...

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.081
but more like this

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:31.651
where each increase of 10 decibels 
sounds twice as loud.

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:34.587
So a motorcycle at 100 decibels

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:36.990
sounds twice as loud as a hairdryer

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.324
at 90 decibels.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:42.362
Which is twice as loud as a 
vacuum cleaner at 80 decibels.

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:44.264
This is Sam.

00:02:44.264 --> 00:02:46.833
He produces Vox Atlas and 
he's going to help me

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:47.934
with a little experiment.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:50.236
Okay, go ahead.

00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:53.673
Bouncing a tennis ball 
on a tennis racket

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.441
averaged around 70 decibels...

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:58.745
which is comparable to the noise level 
of a busy office.

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:01.848
When we measured the sound of 
a pickleball against a paddle...

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.486
it clocked in around 80 decibels.

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:09.822
That's right where sound becomes 
loud enough to be irritating.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.059
Now, obviously, this isn't the most 
scientific experiment

00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:15.195
and of course, tennis matches 
make sound.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:18.731
But the point is pickleball's equipment:

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:21.334
rigid paddles against hollow plastic balls...

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.036
makes a lot of noise.

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.270
"I wake up, 
I hear it."

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.572
"I walk down the steps,
I hear it. "

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:27.307
[imitating clanking]

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:28.675
"As loud as a rock concert."

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:31.177
Mounting noise complaints have 
forced cities and towns

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:34.647
from Victoria, British Columbia to 
Newport, Rhode Island

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:36.783
to come up with temporary solutions

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:38.751
like limited hours for pickleball

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:41.888
and in some places, banning 
the sport altogether.

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:45.091
Both sides of this conflict agree.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.893
Pickleball has to go somewhere.

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.829
Expanding or creating dedicated space
for the sport

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.498
would help alleviate the strain 
on shared spaces

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:55.535
and help isolate the noise issue
pickleball brings.

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:58.404
A wave of developers have already 
cashed in on the demand

00:03:58.404 --> 00:03:59.639
opening state of the art

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:01.841
private pickleball clubs 
across the country.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:04.911
So far, underfunded parks and 
rec departments

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:06.212
have been moving slower

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.413
but progress is being made.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.818
Cincinnati recently opened this free-to-the-public
pickleball facility

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:14.287
a collaboration in between 
community fundraisers...

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:15.855
the Cincinnati Parks Department...

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.625
and the philanthropic Cincinnati Parks Foundation

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:20.827
converted 8 dilapidated tennis courts

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:24.564
into 18 pickleball courts, 
plus 3 dual-line courts

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:26.299
for pickleball and tennis.

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:29.269
What was formerly a mostly ignored space

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:32.705
drew over 13,000 pickleball players 
in 2022.

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:36.175
There's plans like this in the works 
all over the country.

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.646
This one was shared with me 
by the Peninsula Tennis Club.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:44.083
That's where that angry pickleball 
takeover took place in April 2022.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:46.953
Their proposal would convert 
this unused space

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:49.722
away from the tennis courts 
for pickleball courts.

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:53.626
These projects need funding, 
but where pickleball goes

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:55.194
other facilities benefit.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:58.197
Efforts to invest in public 
pickleball courts

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:00.133
are resulting in new volleyball courts...

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.036
basketball courts and 
skateparks too.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.205
But until more courts become available...

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:08.508
and maybe even after they do

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:11.544
pickleball will likely maintain
its DIY spirit.

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:13.513
There are pickleball courts 
around the city

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:14.847
but we could just come in here and play

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:17.116
like not really plan anything in advance
and just find a spot..

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.386
Being able to just like grab a little bag here
and come out

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:23.189
and text my friends and have people show up
and meet new people

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:25.091
is very appealing I think.

